            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Problem</h2>
                    <p>Shows the non-atomic issue when saving data and publishing messages separately - if the broker is down you may lose events.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// ❌ Not atomic - what if message broker fails?
await _dbContext.SaveChangesAsync();        // ✅ Saved
await _messagebus.PublishAsync(event);      // ❌ Broker down = lost event!</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Outbox Solution</h2>
                    <p>Outline of the outbox pattern: persist both data and outbox message in the same DB transaction, then a background worker reliably publishes outbox entries to the broker.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-text">1. Save entity + outbox message in SAME transaction
2. Background process reads outbox table
3. Publishes messages to broker
4. Marks messages as processed

┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│         Database Transaction        │
│  ┌────────┐    ┌─────────────────┐  │
│  │ Orders │    │ OutboxMessages  │  │
│  │  ...   │    │ - OrderPlaced   │  │
│  └────────┘    └─────────────────┘  │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
                    │
                    ▼ Background Worker
              ┌───────────┐
              │  Broker   │
              └───────────┘</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>MassTransit Outbox</h2>
                    <p>MassTransit offers an EF Core outbox integration that saves messages with your DbContext transaction and later flushes them to the bus.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddMassTransit(x =&gt;
{
    x.AddEntityFrameworkOutbox&lt;AppDbContext&gt;(o =&gt;
    {
        o.UseSqlServer();
        o.UseBusOutbox();
    });
    
    x.UsingRabbitMq((context, cfg) =&gt;
    {
        cfg.ConfigureEndpoints(context);
    });
});

// Usage - messages saved with transaction
public async Task PlaceOrderAsync(Order order)
{
    _dbContext.Orders.Add(order);
    
    await _publishEndpoint.Publish(new OrderPlaced(order.Id));
    
    await _dbContext.SaveChangesAsync();  // Both saved atomically
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Outbox ensures at-least-once message delivery.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Messages saved in same transaction as data.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>MassTransit has built-in EF Core outbox.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
            </div>
